  <section class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">United States · Vaccine-Preventable Disease · 2000–2026</div>
    <h1>Measles, <em>back</em> in America.</h1>
    <div class="kicker">
      <span><span class="dot"></span>Eliminated in 2000</span>
      <span><span class="dot"></span>Resurgent in 2025</span>
      <span><span class="dot"></span>Elimination status at risk</span>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      For a generation, measles was a disease the United States had beaten. The country declared it
      <strong>eliminated in 2000</strong> — no continuous, home-grown transmission. Twenty-five years later
      it is spreading again. In <strong>2025 the U.S. logged 2,289 confirmed cases</strong>, the most in any
      year since 1992, and its first measles deaths in a decade. The virus did not change. Our defenses did:
      childhood vaccination has slipped below the level needed to keep measles from catching.
    </p>

