  <section id="rise">
    <div class="eyebrow">The trend line</div>
    <h2 class="sec-title">A quarter-century of <em>near-zero</em> — then a wall.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      After elimination, the U.S. averaged roughly <strong>180 cases a year</strong>, almost all imported by
      unvaccinated travelers and quickly contained. Import sparks that once fizzled now find kindling:
      pockets of under-vaccinated communities where one case becomes hundreds. The 2014 and 2019 spikes were
      warnings. <strong>2025 broke the scale.</strong>
    </p>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-head">
        <span class="t">Confirmed measles cases · United States · 2000–2025</span>
        <span class="s">Source · CDC</span>
      </div>
      <div id="rise-chart" class="chart"></div>
      <div class="cap">
        Post-elimination baseline (2000–2013) hovered near <b>180/yr</b>. Outbreak years — <b>2014 (667)</b>,
        <b>2019 (1,274)</b> — grew larger and closer together, before <b>2025 (2,289)</b>. Hover any year for detail.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ THE MAP ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="map">
    <div class="eyebrow">Where it spread</div>
    <h2 class="sec-title">One county lit the <em>match</em>.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The 2025 resurgence had an epicenter: <strong>Gaines County, Texas</strong>, an under-vaccinated rural
      community where the outbreak began in late January and grew to hundreds of cases — the large majority of
      the year’s national total. From there it jumped to <strong>New Mexico, Kansas, Oklahoma and Ohio</strong>,
      and single cases surfaced in more than 30 states as travelers carried it across the country.
    </p>
    <div class="map-wrap">
