  <!-- ════ THE VACCINATION GAP ═════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="vaccines">
    <div class="eyebrow">The cause</div>
    <h2 class="sec-title">This is a <em>vaccination</em> story.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Measles is among the most contagious diseases known — one case can infect up to 18 others. That is why the
      bar for community protection is so high: <strong>about 95% of people must be immune</strong> to stop it
      spreading. Two MMR doses are ~97% effective. But kindergarten MMR coverage has fallen every year since the
      pandemic, dipping to <strong>92.5% in 2024–25</strong>, while vaccine exemptions hit a record high. The gap
      between 95% and where we are is exactly the room measles needs.
    </p>
    <div class="gap-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head">
          <span class="t">Kindergarten MMR coverage vs. 95% target</span>
          <span class="s">Source · CDC SchoolVaxView</span>
        </div>
        <div id="cov-chart" class="chart short"></div>
        <div class="cap">National MMR coverage among kindergartners has sat <b>below the 95% target every year since 2019–20</b>. Small national dips hide sharp local shortfalls.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="gap-cards">
        <div class="mini"><div class="mv red">~93%</div><div class="ml">of 2025 cases were in people <b>unvaccinated or of unknown status</b></div></div>
        <div class="mini"><div class="mv amber">3.6%</div><div class="ml">Kindergarten exemption rate — a <b>record high</b>, up from 3.3%</div></div>
        <div class="mini"><div class="mv">39</div><div class="ml">States below the <b>95% target</b> in 2024–25 (up from 28)</div></div>
        <div class="mini"><div class="mv">~286K</div><div class="ml">Kindergartners <b>unprotected</b> against measles nationwide</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>Of every 100 measles cases in 2025, roughly <b>93 were people who were not vaccinated</b> — or didn’t know if they were. Measles isn’t back because it got stronger. It’s back because immunity got thinner.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 2025 TIMELINE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="timeline">
    <div class="eyebrow">How 2025 unfolded</div>
    <h2 class="sec-title">From a single county to a <em>national</em> alarm.</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="when">Late January 2025</div><div class="what">An outbreak begins in <b>Gaines County, West Texas</b>, a rural community with low vaccination uptake. It spreads through unvaccinated children and families.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="when">February–March 2025</div><div class="what">The cluster crosses into <b>New Mexico</b>, then <b>Kansas</b> and <b>Oklahoma</b>. The U.S. records its <b>first measles deaths in a decade</b> — two unvaccinated children in Texas and an unvaccinated adult in New Mexico.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="when">Spring 2025</div><div class="what">By mid-April, <b>27+ states</b> report at least one case; new outbreaks flare in <b>Ohio</b>. Most cases are travel-linked sparks landing in under-vaccinated pockets.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="when">Mid-2025</div><div class="what">National cases pass <b>1,000</b>, then keep climbing — the worst year since the 1990s and a direct threat to the <b>elimination status</b> the U.S. has held since 2000.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="when">Year-end 2025</div><div class="what"><b>2,289 confirmed cases across 48 outbreaks.</b> The West Texas outbreak is declared over, but the pattern is set — and 2026 opens with new clusters already reported.</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

